## One loop, one model

Wyrd records one typed model of your AI stack and you work the same loop against
it the whole way: **define** what exists, **govern** who can do what, **observe**
what happened, **evaluate** whether it still works, and **react** when it
doesn't. Each turn writes back to the same model, so the next turn starts from
ground truth instead of a fresh guess.

<dl class="wyrd-defs">
  <dt data-kind="data" data-step="01">Define</dt>
  <dd>Declare every component — model, prompt, agent, service, dataset, policy, eval — as a typed, versioned Card.</dd>

  <dt data-kind="policy" data-step="02">Govern</dt>
  <dd>Bind identity to Cards and gate access with policy before anything runs.</dd>

  <dt data-kind="source" data-step="03">Observe</dt>
  <dd>Record observations across the whole stack and tie them back to the Card versions that produced them.</dd>

  <dt data-kind="eval" data-step="04">Evaluate</dt>
  <dd>Score behavior against declared expectations and track drift over time.</dd>

  <dt data-kind="trigger" data-step="05">React</dt>
  <dd>Close the loop with triggers and operators that respond to what Wyrd observes.</dd>
</dl>

## How it connects

Every component is a Card. A Card references the Cards it depends on through a
`CardRef`, and Wyrd derives lineage from those references — you never author the
graph by hand. Here a support-triage Agent pins the exact Prompt version it runs:

```yaml
apiVersion: wyrd/v1
metadata:
  space: support
  name: support-triage
  version: "0.3.0"
kind: Agent
spec:
  prompt:
    kind: Prompt
    name: triage-instructions
    space: support
    version: "1.2.0"
  tools:
    - escalate-ticket
    - lookup-order
```

Apply it and Wyrd versions the Agent, resolves the `CardRef`, and links the two
in the lineage graph:

```bash
wyrd apply -f support-triage.yaml
```

<CardTileGrid />

Every kind is a Card; the `CardRef` values between them are how Wyrd derives
lineage.

## Built for agents and people

Wyrd is agent-first and headless. MCP, CLI, and HTTP are first-class surfaces,
backed by typed schemas, stable errors, and machine-readable docs at
[`/llms.txt`](/llms.txt) and [`/llms-full.txt`](/llms-full.txt). A careful agent
and a human reason about the same typed contracts — no surface is privileged
over another.

## Where your code stays

Per core doctrine #7, **Wyrd reads, it does not push**. Wyrd records, validates,
versions, links, governs, installs, observes, and exposes — but your inference,
training, and agent loops run in your own runtime. There is no execution
handoff and no runtime lock-in.
